TaskTrack – Software Requirements Specification
Student Assignment Management System | Version 1.0 | Prepared by the TaskTrack development team
1. Functional Requirements
Priority key: High = must have for version 1.0, Medium = should have, Low = nice to have.
1.1 Account Access (Medium)
• FR-1.1: The system shall allow a student to register and log in so that their assignments are stored privately. (Medium; may be simplified to a single-user mode if the team decides accounts are out of scope.)
• FR-1.2: The system shall show a student only their own assignments and courses.
1.2 Assignment Management (High)
• FR-2.1: The system shall allow a student to create an assignment with a title, course name, description, and due date.
• FR-2.2: The system shall require a title, course, and due date, and shall allow the description to be left blank.
• FR-2.3: The system shall reject invalid input (for example, an empty title or an incorrectly formatted date) and display a clear error message.
• FR-2.4: The system shall allow a student to edit any field of an existing assignment.
• FR-2.5: The system shall allow a student to delete an assignment.
• FR-2.6: The system shall ask the student to confirm before an assignment is permanently deleted.
• FR-2.7: The system shall save all assignment changes so that they are still present after the student closes and reopens the application.
1.3 Upcoming Assignments View (High)
• FR-3.1: The system shall display assignments sorted by due date, with the earliest deadline first.
• FR-3.2: The system shall show each assignment's title, course, and due date in the list.
• FR-3.3: The system shall visually distinguish overdue assignments from other assignments.
• FR-3.4: The system shall show the full details of an assignment when the student selects it.
1.4 Completion Status (High)
• FR-4.1: The system shall allow a student to mark an assignment as completed.
• FR-4.2: The system shall allow a student to change a completed assignment back to incomplete.
• FR-4.3: The system shall visually distinguish completed assignments from incomplete ones.
• FR-4.4: The system shall update dashboard counts immediately when an assignment's status changes.
1.5 Course Organization (High)
• FR-5.1: The system shall allow a student to assign each assignment to a course.
• FR-5.2: The system shall allow a student to view the assignments for a single selected course.
• FR-5.3: The system shall allow a student to add a new course while creating an assignment or from a course list.
• FR-5.4: The system shall list all of the student's courses and allow the student to filter by any one of them.
1.6 Dashboard (High)
• FR-6.1: The system shall provide a dashboard showing the number of pending, completed, and overdue assignments.
• FR-6.2: The system shall classify an assignment as overdue when its due date has passed and it is not completed.
• FR-6.3: The system shall allow the student to open the list of assignments behind each dashboard count.
• FR-6.4: The dashboard shall show the next assignment(s) due so the student can see what to work on next.
2. Non-Functional Requirements
2.1 Usability
• NFR-1: A first-time student shall be able to add an assignment without instructions or training.
• NFR-2: Core actions (add, edit, delete, mark complete) shall be reachable within two clicks or taps from the main screen.
• NFR-3: The interface shall use consistent layouts, labels, and colors throughout.
2.2 Reliability and Data Integrity
• NFR-4: Saved assignment data shall not be lost or altered incorrectly during normal use.
• NFR-5: Assignments shall always be displayed in the correct deadline order.
• NFR-6: The system shall show an error message, rather than failing silently, if saving or loading data fails.
2.3 Performance
• NFR-7: Pages and list views shall load within 3 seconds under normal conditions for a typical student (up to about 200 assignments).
• NFR-8: Adding, editing, or completing an assignment shall be reflected on screen within 1 second.
2.4 Compatibility and Responsiveness
• NFR-9: The application shall work correctly in the latest versions of Chrome, Firefox, Safari, and Edge.
• NFR-10: The layout shall adapt to different screen sizes, including phones and tablets, through the browser (no native mobile app).
2.5 Security and Privacy
• NFR-11: If accounts are implemented, passwords shall be stored securely (hashed, never as plain text).
• NFR-12: User input shall be validated and sanitized to prevent common attacks such as script injection.
2.6 Maintainability
• NFR-13: Code shall be organized into clear front-end, back-end, and data layers and documented well enough for any team member to work on it.
• NFR-14: The team shall use version control and review changes before merging.
